WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:03.500
THE COMPANY THAT RUNS THE WORLD:
Who’s the most powerful company in the world?

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:08.490
Most people would probably say Google, Facebook,
Apple, or Amazon.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:13.690
But, what if I told you that there was a singular
company behind all of these.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:19.670
In fact, this singular company not only controls
big tech, but they actually control the entire

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:24.400
corporate world, yet you’ve probably never
even heard their name.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.980
This company goes by the name SAP and they
are the #1 enterprise application provider

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.640
in the entire world.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:38.050
They offer CRM solutions, SaaS solutions,
SCM solutions, and of course ERP solutions.

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:43.899
And virtually every company that you can think
of relies on SAP for their backend solutions.

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:52.010
This includes 98% of the 100 most valued brands,
86% of the Fortune 500, and 92% of the Forbes

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:53.260
2000.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.289
And it’s not just corporations that dependon
SAP either.

00:00:57.289 --> 00:01:02.219
Some of the most poweful governments around
the world also rely on SAP.

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:08.670
This includes the US, Canada, India, the UK,
France, Australia, and many more.

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:14.850
SAP’s customers are responsible for 87%
of total global commerce which works out to

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:17.440
$46 trillion.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.800
Considering this stronghold on the enterprise
market, I don’t think you’d be surprised

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:26.960
to hear that by nature SAP also has a stronghold
on the consumer market.

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:33.460
In fact, 77% of the entire world’s transactional
revenue goes through SAP.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:37.960
This translates to a whopping $16 trillion
per year.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:43.189
With so much power, you would think that SAP
would be up to no good similar to some of

00:01:43.189 --> 00:01:45.460
the financial giants that run the world.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:51.990
But, somehow, in their 50 year history, SAP
has maintained a relatively clean slate.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.759
Really, the only bad things that you can find
about the company are that they’ve had some

00:01:56.759 --> 00:02:01.539
intellectual property lawsuits from competitors
and they’ve had a few cases of bribery.

00:02:01.539 --> 00:02:07.289
There’s also a community that thinks that
SAP software is overpriced and not actually

00:02:07.289 --> 00:02:10.390
that good, but that’s not even an evil offense.

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:16.101
Keep in mind, SAP could literally blackmail
the entire world or run any company that they

00:02:16.101 --> 00:02:17.700
want out of business.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:23.760
So, here’s the story of SAP and how they
became the most powerful company that you’ve

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.270
never heard of.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:31.180
HUMBLE ORIGINS:
Taking a look back, the story of SAP dates

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.410
back to the early 1970s ironically to Xerox.

00:02:35.410 --> 00:02:40.470
As you may know from one of our previous videos,
Xerox basically invented the computer, the

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:45.190
laptop, the internet, and programming and
gave it all away.

00:02:45.190 --> 00:02:49.330
It turns out that you can add SAP onto that
list as well.

00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:54.280
The story goes that when Xerox exited the
computer hardware manufacturing industry in

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:59.400
1971, they asked IBM to migrate their business
systems to IBM technology.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:05.330
And to do this, Xerox offered IBM the rights
to their SDS/SAPE software which was at the

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:07.299
time worth about 80 grand.

00:03:07.299 --> 00:03:11.530
That’s equivalent to about $588,000 today.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:16.549
Five German engineers from IBM’s AI department
were initially assigned to this project, but

00:03:16.549 --> 00:03:21.489
due to some unknown corporate reasons, they
would eventually be switched to another project.

00:03:21.489 --> 00:03:24.459
These five engineers, however, didn’t want
to switch.

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:28.569
They had witnessed first hand just how much
companies were willing to pay to outsource

00:03:28.569 --> 00:03:29.900
backend solutions.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:35.430
So, instead of switching to another IBM project
as instructed, these five would quit IBM and

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:40.550
establish their own company called System
Analysis and Program Development or SAP in

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:41.950
June of 1972.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:47.280
Their first product was quite straight forward
and had to do with tracking employee punchcards.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.140
IBM was the industry leader within this industry,
so these five were pretty familiar with the

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.720
current systems and their capabilities.

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:00.640
One of the biggest shortfalls of the IBM punch
card system was that it recorded data mechanically.

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.570
And every night, someone would have to process
this data manually.

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:09.750
For obvious reasons, this was undesirable,
and SAP aimed to eliminate this hassle with

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.000
their RF system.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:16.780
Unlike IBM’s systems, the RF system processed
punch card data electronically and automatically

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.190
stored the data onto a enterprise wide database.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:24.610
This made the data easily accessible for anyone
within the organization whether its the accounting

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:26.540
team or the payroll team.

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:32.139
While this was convenient, it also required
that the company maintain a database.

00:04:32.139 --> 00:04:37.620
This might be viable for someone like IBM
or Xerox, but the local factory had no idea

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:40.560
how to operate or maintain a database.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:46.440
So, in addition to the system, SAP also offered
regular maintenance and upgrades and this

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:48.380
is where the money was really at.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:53.070
At first, this was actually super easy given
that there was plenty of downtime.

00:04:53.070 --> 00:04:56.639
These systems were only used during the day
monday through friday.

00:04:56.639 --> 00:05:00.479
So, you could upgrade the system during nights
and on the weekend.

00:05:00.479 --> 00:05:05.789
But nowadays, there isn’t nearly as big
of a window given that downtime is extremely

00:05:05.789 --> 00:05:07.610
expensive in the modern world.

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:12.419
So, SAP has had to become quite creative when
it comes to maintenance and upgrades.

00:05:12.419 --> 00:05:18.111
Anyway, SAP would further refine this system
and create their first flagship product: the

00:05:18.111 --> 00:05:19.830
SAP R/1.

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:24.669
SAP’s first customer was a chemical company
in Germany, but it didn’t take them long

00:05:24.669 --> 00:05:27.280
to onboard a bunch of local companies.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:34.419
And it didn’t take long for SAP to become
the go to company for all in one backend solutions.

00:05:34.419 --> 00:05:40.000
FIERCE EXPANSION:
Over the years, SAP has grown their product

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:41.009
line aggressively.

00:05:41.009 --> 00:05:46.690
In fact, they currently offer 309 products
today, but most of this can be watered down

00:05:46.690 --> 00:05:53.479
into 5 main categories starting with SAP ERP
or SAP Enterprise Resource Planning.

00:05:53.479 --> 00:05:58.790
This is really the core of SAP and it covers
all of the main functions that any business

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:00.020
would need.

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:04.660
This includes supply chain management, product
lifecycle management, human capital management,

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:07.270
financial management, and customer relationship
management software.

00:06:07.270 --> 00:06:10.169
All of these accomplish exactly what they
sound like.

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:14.580
The supply chain management software, for
example, helps companies keep track of inventory,

00:06:14.580 --> 00:06:19.780
analyze demand, create projections of supply
requirements, manage logistics, and so on.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:23.880
Similarly, the financial management software
makes it easy to track expenditures, calculate

00:06:23.880 --> 00:06:28.180
taxes, manage your assets, and ensure that
you’re compliant with government regulations.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:33.030
If a company tells you that they’re using
SAP, they're probably talking about one of

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:34.840
their ERP products.

00:06:34.840 --> 00:06:39.320
If not, it’s more than likely that they’re
talking about SAP’s second most popular

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:42.410
offering which is SAP S/4 HANA.

00:06:42.410 --> 00:06:47.849
This is actually a relatively new product
from SAP having been released in 2015.

00:06:47.849 --> 00:06:54.020
SAP S/4 HANA is basically just an upgraded
version of SAP ERP.

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:58.800
It seeks to use more advanced technologies
such as parallel processing to make SAP ERP

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.020
more efficient for larger corporations.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:05.289
This allows for SAP’s systems to operate
on a virtually realtime basis.

00:07:05.289 --> 00:07:11.210
But, if SAP S/4 HANA is substantially better
than SAP ERP, wouldn’t every corporation

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:13.270
just use S/4 HANA.

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:18.780
Well no because the better performance comes
along with a crap load of extra responsibilities.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:24.229
Essentially, you need a bunch of in house
IT staff that ensures S/4 HANA operates as

00:07:24.229 --> 00:07:25.229
intended.

00:07:25.229 --> 00:07:29.930
For companies like Apple and Google who already
have a bunch of IT staff, springing for this

00:07:29.930 --> 00:07:31.080
is a no brainer.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:35.430
But, for a company like Coca Cola, the pitch
isn’t as appealing.

00:07:35.430 --> 00:07:39.470
For companies that don’t want to do a bunch
of inhouse management, the most appealing

00:07:39.470 --> 00:07:42.780
option may be SAP S/4 HANA Cloud.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:48.740
This offering is basically identical to SAP
S/4 HANA except for one crucial difference.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:53.699
Instead of running on in house databases and
infrastructure, this runs on the cloud.

00:07:53.699 --> 00:07:58.400
Companies can choose to deploy the software
onto AWS or Microsoft Azure.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:04.310
This substantially reduces the amount of maintenance
that the companies have to do, but in exchange,

00:08:04.310 --> 00:08:07.610
companies give up much of the flexibility
and customization that are characteristic

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:09.349
of the other offerings.

00:08:09.349 --> 00:08:14.870
Moving onto the last two product categories
from SAP, we have SAP Business One and SAP

00:08:14.870 --> 00:08:16.159
Business ByDesign.

00:08:16.159 --> 00:08:20.580
These are essentially lite versions of SAP
that are tailored to smaller businesses.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:26.020
SAP Business One, for example, is meant for
companies with up to 350 employees while SAP

00:08:26.020 --> 00:08:30.139
Business ByDesign is meant for companies with
250 to 1,500 employees.

00:08:30.139 --> 00:08:34.990
But, at the core of it, these offerings accomplish
the same things, just for a different set

00:08:34.990 --> 00:08:36.360
of companies.

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:41.910
As you can see, SAP basically covers all business
needs for all business sizes, so it’s not

00:08:41.910 --> 00:08:45.980
surprising that they’re so dominant within
the industry.

00:08:45.980 --> 00:08:51.700
THE COST:
Hearing all this, you’re probably wondering

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:54.360
just how much all of this costs.

00:08:54.360 --> 00:09:00.320
SAP does post some pricing data for their
products, but really, every SAP deployment

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.800
is handled on a case by case basis, especially
for larger companies.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:09.810
So, it’s unlikely that any two companies
pay the exact same amount, but here’s a

00:09:09.810 --> 00:09:13.020
ballpark estimate of what a company may expect
to pay.

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:17.019
The average ERP instance uses about 2 to 4
TB.

00:09:17.019 --> 00:09:21.079
The average HANA deployment is supposed to
reduce this substantially.

00:09:21.079 --> 00:09:24.550
If we assume a 50% reduction, we get an instance
of about 1-2 TB.

00:09:24.550 --> 00:09:31.810
According to brightworkresearch, this translates
to an upfront cost of $6.2 million along with

00:09:31.810 --> 00:09:35.560
an annual maintenance fee of $1.24 million.

00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:39.670
Larger companies can likely expect to pay
an upfront fee in the tens of millions along

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:42.830
with an annual maintenance fee of several
million dollars.

00:09:42.830 --> 00:09:46.420
And that’s just the fee that you have to
pay SAP.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:51.670
For most deployments, you need in house staff
and this is very much true for the juggernauts.

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:56.200
Amazon, for example, currently has 42 openings
for SAP related roles.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:58.540
Google has 28 openings for SAP.

00:09:58.540 --> 00:10:01.560
And Apple has 81 openings for SAP.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:04.100
And that’s just the open roles.

00:10:04.100 --> 00:10:08.600
All of these companies likely have several
hundred if not over a thousand full time staff

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:10.730
that just maintain these systems.

00:10:10.730 --> 00:10:15.561
And given that solutions architects demand
quite a high salary, ERP likely costs these

00:10:15.561 --> 00:10:20.110
companies well into the hundreds of millions
if not billions every single year.

00:10:20.110 --> 00:10:23.940
But, the truth is that there’s no way around
this.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:28.720
Really, the only other options you have are
to use Oracle who charge the same amounts

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:34.079
or you can develop your own in house systems,
but usually that’s even more expensive.

00:10:34.079 --> 00:10:37.769
Not to mention, it’ll just distract the
company from being able to focus on growing

00:10:37.769 --> 00:10:39.140
the core business.

00:10:39.140 --> 00:10:44.830
So, basically every entity that you can think
of end up using SAP, Oracle, or some combination

00:10:44.830 --> 00:10:46.000
of the two.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.870
Given all this power, you would think that
SAP would be up to no good, but as we discussed,

00:10:51.870 --> 00:10:56.230
while there is some discussion regarding whether
SAP’s products are actually worth it or

00:10:56.230 --> 00:11:01.050
not, there’s not much discussion regarding
the morality of the company as a whole.

00:11:01.050 --> 00:11:04.220
If anything, it’s actually the exact opposite.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:08.870
SAP’s employees are extremely happy with
the company and it’s leadership.

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:15.430
SAP has a total of 26,000 reviews on glassdoor
with an average rating of 4.4 stars.

00:11:15.430 --> 00:11:22.600
Additionally, 92% of employees would recommend
SAP to a friend and 91% approve of the CEO.

00:11:22.600 --> 00:11:27.889
On top of this, 68% of employees would not
leave SAP even if they were offered more money

00:11:27.889 --> 00:11:30.750
and 83% are excited to go to work every day.

00:11:30.750 --> 00:11:36.490
Clearly, SAP is doing something right, and
this is reflected in their financials as well.

00:11:36.490 --> 00:11:41.521
SAP regularly pulls in $30 billion per year
and they boast net income of $4 billion per

00:11:41.521 --> 00:11:42.521
year.

00:11:42.521 --> 00:11:47.630
But, these numbers are nothing in comparison
to the true power that they have.

00:11:47.630 --> 00:11:54.360
THE SAP STRONGHOLD:
In the end, we often complain about x or y

00:11:54.360 --> 00:12:00.240
company being a monopoly and having way too
much power, but oftentimes some of the most

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:04.370
powerful companies are background companies
that we’ve never heard of.

00:12:04.370 --> 00:12:09.440
SAP is very much of these companies as they
basically control all of the business software

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:11.639
used by the entire world.

00:12:11.639 --> 00:12:17.760
If this much power fell into the wrong hands,
who knows what could happen, but so far, SAP

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:21.760
has proven to be very much qualified to handle
this responsibility.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:25.340
Looking forward, it doesn’t look like SAP
is going anywhere.

00:12:25.340 --> 00:12:31.019
In fact, if anything, it seems like companies
are shifting from Oracle to SAP for greater

00:12:31.019 --> 00:12:34.829
flexibility and to avoid Oracle’s antics
with intellectual property.

00:12:34.829 --> 00:12:40.490
So, you better start keeping tabs on this
SAP because they’re here to stay and they’re

00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:44.389
only growing stronger by the day, but that’s
just what I think.

00:12:44.389 --> 00:12:46.389
Were you familiar with SAP?

00:12:46.389 --> 00:12:47.389
Comment that down below.

00:12:47.389 --> 00:12:52.360
Also, drop a like you hope SAP gets some more
competition.

00:12:52.360 --> 00:12:56.260
And of course, consider checking out our discord
community to suggest future video ideas and

00:12:56.260 --> 00:13:02.750
consider subscribing to see more questions
logically answered.

